  "workflowName": "impeccable-full-critique-2",
  "script": "export const meta = { name: \"impeccable-full-critique-2\", description: \"Dual-assessment design critique + technical/copy/responsive audits\", phases: [{ id: \"assess\", title: \"Parallel assessments\" }] };\nconst COMMON = `Project: /home/alex/Projects/mindscape-site — Next.js 16 App Router marketing site for \"Mindscape Psychedelic Institute\", a premium psychedelic-training educational institution. Mode: PERSUADE (visitors decide and act: apply, enroll, book a call). Design authority: DESIGN.md (deep navy #0D2B45 foundation, teal #4B778D secondary, gold #D4A84B reserved for primary CTAs, Playfair Display headings + Inter body, institutional gravitas with approachable warmth, no bounce easing, tinted shadows). Tailwind CSS 4 via @tailwindcss/postcss, plus tailwind.config.ts and src/app/globals.css.\nKey surfaces: src/app/page.tsx (home), about, apply, coaching, compare, contact, courses, courses/[slug] (CourseDetailContent + components/), faculty/[slug], podcast, research, links, not-found, layout.tsx, globals.css, src/components/ (layout/Header, layout/Footer, chat/ChatWidget, conversion/, sections/, magicui/).\nYou have read access to all files and can run read-only shell commands. Do NOT edit any files. Cite file:line for every finding.`;\n\nphase(\"assess\");\nconst [designReview, detector, techAudit, copyAudit, responsiveAudit] = await parallel([\n  () => agent(`${COMMON}\n\nYou are ASSESSMENT A: an award-winning design director performing a holistic design critique. You have NOT seen any automated detector output; form independent judgments first.\n\nRead the homepage, courses index, courses/[slug] detail, apply, coaching, contact, Header, Footer, ChatWidget, globals.css/tokens. Then evaluate:\n1. DESIGN SPECIFICITY: is the visual language grounded in THIS product (psychedelic education, clinical + contemplative), or could an unrelated SaaS use it unchanged? Verdict + evidence.\n2. NIELSEN HEURISTICS: score all 10, 0-4 each, with one key issue per heuristic. n/a allowed where the heuristic doesn't apply to a marketing site.\n3. COGNITIVE LOAD: decision points with >4 visible options; competing CTAs; jargon.\n4. EMOTIONAL JOURNEY: peak-end, valleys, reassurance at high-stakes moments (applying, paying a deposit for psychedelic training — trust-critical).\n5. PERSONAS: Jordan (skeptical first-timer), Riley (stress-tester), Casey (distracted mobile user) — red flags each.\n6. Return: specificity verdict, heuristic score table, 2-3 strengths, 3-5 priority issues ranked, minor observations, 2 provocative questions for the team.\nBe specific and evidence-based; quote actual copy and components.`, { label: \"A:design-review\", phase: \"assess\", retries: 1 }),\n\n  () => agent(`${COMMON}\n\nYou are ASSESSMENT B: deterministic detector evidence. No browser automation exists in this harness, so browser visualization is unavailable — note that as a skipped step with reason, and rely on the CLI scan.\n\nRun exactly: node /home/alex/.pi/agent/skills/impeccable/scripts/detect.mjs --json src/app src/components\nIf that fails, try narrower targets (src/app, then src/components). Report the raw JSON findings COMPLETE: total count, per-rule counts, and every finding with file:line and rule name. Then classify: which findings are likely false positives given the Next.js/Tailwind context (verify 3-5 by reading the cited code). Return: exit code, counts, full findings list, false-positive analysis.`, { label: \"B:detector\", phase: \"assess\", retries: 1 }),\n\n  () => agent(`${COMMON}\n\nYou are the TECHNICAL AUDIT agent. Code-level checks only, no redesign opinions. Score 5 dimensions 0-4 and list findings as P0/P1/P2/P3 with file:line, impact, WCAG/standard violated, and concrete fix:\n1. ACCESSIBILITY: contrast (compute ratios for teal #4B778D and gold #D4A84B text on navy/white — do the math), missing ARIA/labels (audit ChatWidget, Header mobile menu, forms in apply/waitlist-form/deposit modals), keyboard nav/focus indicators, heading hierarchy per page, alt text on all images, form labels, prefers-reduced-motion handling.\n2. PERFORMANCE: next/image vs raw <img>, lazy loading, bundle-heavy imports, client components that could be server, layout thrash, expensive blur/animations, font loading strategy in layout.tsx.\n3. THEMING: hard-coded hex colors vs tokens (grep for hex colors in src), token consistency, dark-mode gaps.\n4. RESPONSIVE: fixed widths, touch targets <44px (measure padding on buttons/links in Header, mobile CTA bar, cards), horizontal overflow risks, breakpoint coverage.\n5. IMPLEMENTATION INTEGRITY: placeholder/lorem/TODO content, dead code, drift from DESIGN.md rules (gold only for CTAs, Playfair for h1-h4, etc).\nAlso list POSITIVE findings. Return: score table (x/20), rating band, findings by severity, systemic patterns.`, { label: \"C:tech-audit\", phase: \"assess\", retries: 1 }),\n\n  () => agent(`${COMMON}\n\nYou are the UX COPY auditor (clarify pass). Read ALL user-facing copy across every page and component, including: Header/Footer nav labels, all page headings/intros, CTA button labels, the apply form, waitlist-form, deposit-intent-modal, deposit-qr-modal, status-toggle, faq-item, ChatWidget (its prompts, errors, empty states), contact page, not-found, LegalPopups, API route user-facing error messages (src/app/api/*/route.ts).\nAudience: clinicians, facilitators, serious students evaluating high-stakes psychedelic training (trust, safety, cost anxiety).\nFind: ambiguous verbs/labels, jargon assumed, vague outcomes (\"Learn More\", \"Submit\", \"Click here\"), missing consequence/recovery/timing info, inconsistent terminology & capitalization (e.g. program vs course vs cohort), error messages that blame or expose internals, empty/success states without next action, placeholder-as-label form patterns, tone mismatches at high-stakes moments (payment, application).\nFor EACH finding give: file:line, current copy (quoted), why it's unclear for this audience, and a concrete rewrite that preserves factual meaning and brand voice (institutional gravitas, warmth). Group by P0 (blocks/misleads), P1 (decision-point confusion), P2 (polish). Also note what's already strong.`, { label: \"D:copy-audit\", phase: \"assess\", retries: 1 }),\n\n  () => agent(`${COMMON}\n\nYou are the RESPONSIVE/ADAPT auditor. Evaluate how the experience adapts across devices: phones (320-430px, one-handed thumb use, notches), tablets (768-1024, touch+pointer), desktop (1024+, hover, keyboard), wide (1440+), and landscape phones. Read the layout code and reason concretely; do not guess.\nCheck: Header nav collapse behavior & hamburger target size; CourseMobileStickyCtaBar (thumb zone, safe-area-inset padding, overlap with content/footer); hero sections at 320px (headline clamp, CTA stacking); grids (courses, faculty, testimonials) — when do they reflow, do cards get too narrow between breakpoints; typography scale vs DESIGN.md scale (h1 2.5rem mobile); touch targets <44px anywhere (measure padding+font on nav links, footer links, icon buttons, chat widget trigger, accordion/faq toggles); hover-dependent interactions without touch alternative; horizontal overflow risks (fixed widths, long URLs, tables); images with fixed dimensions; safe-area env() usage and viewport-fit in layout.tsx metadata; form usability on mobile (input font-size <16px causes iOS zoom); sticky/fixed elements stacking conflicts (sticky CTA bar vs ChatWidget vs LegalPopups on small screens).\nFor each finding: file:line, device/context affected, severity P0-P2, concrete fix with actual CSS/Tailwind change. Note what already adapts well.`, { label: \"E:responsive-audit\", phase: \"assess\", retries: 1 })\n]);\n\nreturn { designReview, detector, techAudit, copyAudit, responsiveAudit };",
